.TH split "" "Command"
.SH NAME
split \- split a large file into smaller files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsplit [\-\fInlines\fB] [\fIinfile \fB[\fIoutfile\fB]]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
split -- split a large file into smaller files
Usage:	split [ -nlines ] [ infile [ outfile ] ]
'infile' of '-' or no 'infile' means standard input.
'outfile' defaults to 'x'.
.HE
.B split
copies
.I infile
(or the standard input, if
.I infile
is omitted or is
.BR \- )
into a series of new files, each holding at most
.I nlines
lines of the input (the last file may hold fewer).
The new files are named by appending
.BR aa ,
.BR ab ,
.BR ac ,
and so on, in turn, to
.I outfile
(default
.BR x ),
so that the first file is
.IR outfile aa,
the second
.IR outfile ab,
and so on through
.IR outfile zz.
.PP
The default number of lines per output file is 1000; this may be
changed by giving
.BI \- nlines
as the first argument.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B split
complains and exits if
.I infile
cannot be opened, if an output file cannot be created, or if more
than 676 output files (through
.IR outfile zz)
would be needed.
.SH NOTES
.I nlines
must be a positive number; anything else is rejected with a usage
message.
